"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[81287],{81287:function(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"content":"\\n<center>    \\n<BookCard \\n          cover=\\"/assets/snippets/books/malice.png\\"\\n          title=\\"Malice\\"\\n          author=\\"Keigo Higashino\\"\\n          publisher=\\"Hyundae Munhak\\"\\n          publishedDate=\\"2008-07-25\\"\\n          isbn=\\"9788972754190\\"\\n          rating=5\\n          tags=\\"mystery\\"\\n        />\\n</center>\\n\\n## Review\\nThe biggest feature of this work is that it boldly discards the question ```who is the culprit``` and doggedly digs into the question of ```why did they commit the crime```.  \\nThe culprit is revealed early in the novel, and the readers\' <mark>finding the culprit</mark> is intentionally removed to force the reader\'s gaze to a more complex and fundamental mystery: <mark>tracking the motive</mark>.  \\n<br>\\nIt unfolds in a unique format of presenting the records of two characters interchangeably rather than an omniscient author\'s perspective.  \\nThe intersection of the culprit\'s ```hand-written account``` and Detective Kaga\'s ```investigation record``` is a core theme of the novel, exploring the truth and fiction inherent in the act of <mark>recording</mark> itself.  \\nThe reader must discern the greater truth hidden behind the recorded truth through two conflicting texts.  \\n<mark>Malice</mark> means pure hatred that seeks only to negate and destroy the opponent\'s existence itself without any specific occasion or reason.  \\n<br>\\nPersonally, it\'s a work I like."}')}}]);